From: PS3 Custom Toolchain
Subject: [PATCH] rs6000: a float load or store keeps a displacement that is a multiple of 4 (cell64lv2)

Under the 64-bit address model (0059) the vectoriser's realignment IV is
base+15, and GCC folds the +1 that reaches an aligned element back into the
displacement: `stfs 0,1(r10)` writes base+16.  The access is aligned, but
the displacement is not, and a post-link check of the binary (applications
use one: a misaligned lfs/stfs ends the PPU thread silently on a PS3) cannot
tell the two apart without tracing the base register.  Before 0059 every
address was materialised separately (addi; clrldi; stfs 0(rX)), so the
displacement was always 0.

On cell64lv2 an SFmode or DFmode offset address whose displacement is not a
multiple of 4 is no longer a legitimate offset address, so the odd part is
added into the base: one addi.  Integer accesses are not affected.

---
diff --git a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.cc b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.cc
--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.cc
@@ -8847,6 +8847,16 @@ rs6000_legitimate_offset_address_p (machine_mode mode, rtx x,
 
   offset = INTVAL (XEXP (x, 1));
   extra = 0;
+#ifdef POWERPC_CELL64LV2
+  /* A float load or store keeps a displacement that is a multiple of 4.
+     The effective address may still be aligned with an odd displacement
+     (the vectoriser's realignment IV is base+15, and stfs 1(base+15)
+     writes base+16), but a post-link check cannot tell that from a
+     misaligned access, which ends the PPU thread on this target.  Moving
+     the odd part into the base costs one addi.  */
+  if ((mode == SFmode || mode == DFmode) && (offset & 3) != 0)
+    return false;
+#endif
   switch (mode)
     {
     case E_DFmode:
